00:02:03.228 --> 00:02:05.500
a load balancer as well.

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:08.020
So let's get started with
network load balancers.

00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:08.520
Right?

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:10.312
So these are load
balancers that are really

00:02:10.312 --> 00:02:11.660
operating at the network layer.

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:11.850
Right?

00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:13.050
So when I say that,
they're operating

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:14.640
at layer three and layer four.

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:15.300
Right?

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:17.440
These are external
load balancers.

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:17.940
Right?

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:19.773
So we talked about
external versus internal.

00:02:19.773 --> 00:02:21.660
These are facing the internet.

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:23.950
And these are regional
load balancers.

00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:25.560
So what I mean by
that is they operate

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:27.730
within Google Cloud regions.

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:29.910
So in this example,
I'm showing a number

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:32.430
of users that are
running a number of apps

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:35.308
that are running inside GCP.

00:02:35.308 --> 00:02:37.600
In each of these they're
running in a different region.

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:41.040
So you can see MyApp.com
is running in US West.

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:44.400
Test.com is running in Europe.

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:46.050
And Travel.com is
running in Asia.

00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:49.200
So those are various
regions for Google Cloud.

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:50.700
In each case, I
have a load balancer

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:54.000
running in that region
that is receiving traffic

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:55.980
from customers on the
internet and pushing them

00:02:55.980 --> 00:03:01.210
to, in this case, MyApp.com
or Test.com on the backside.

00:03:01.210 --> 00:03:03.018
So let me go over
sort of network load

00:03:03.018 --> 00:03:04.560
balancers at a very
high level so you

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:06.852
can understand the features
that come along with these.

00:03:06.852 --> 00:03:08.400
So they are regional.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:09.520
They are highly available.

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:11.370
They do run across
multiple zones.

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:15.070
They are designed to really load
balance TCP and UDP traffic.

00:03:15.070 --> 00:03:17.680
So as we'll talk about next,
our layer seven load balancers,

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:19.680
these are really operating
at the network layer.

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:20.220
Right?

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:21.887
So when you're dealing
with higher layer

00:03:21.887 --> 00:03:24.930
protocols like SSL or
HTP, all that stuff

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:25.980
just passes through here.

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:26.480
Right?

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:28.140
So if you're running
things like SSL,

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:29.670
you are back ends need to
be able to terminate that,

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:32.203
because the load balancers
aren't going to do it for you.

00:03:32.203 --> 00:03:33.870
One of the common
questions we get asked

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:35.880
is about client side IP.

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:38.160
In terms of the
network load balancers

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:40.062
the client IP is preserved.

00:03:40.062 --> 00:03:42.270
So when we talk about the
layer seven load balancers,

00:03:42.270 --> 00:03:42.960
that isn't the case.

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:45.300
And we get into the concept we
call x-forward for headers, in

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:46.920
and ways that you
can manipulate that.

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:48.720
But in terms of this, the
network load balancer,

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:50.670
that client IP is
actually passed directly

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:51.520
through the load balancer.

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:52.895
So it's not
terminating sessions.

00:03:52.895 --> 00:03:54.840
It's not changing anything.

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:58.050
Because of that, you can
actually use the VPC firewall

00:03:58.050 --> 00:04:00.300
constructs that we talked
about in our previous video

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:02.410
to actually enforce
security policy.

00:04:02.410 --> 00:04:05.130
So if there is a regional
IP block that you don't want

00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:07.307
to allow, or something that
you want to white list,

00:04:07.307 --> 00:04:09.390
you can use the firewall
rules to actually control

00:04:09.390 --> 00:04:11.010
that because the
client IPs are being

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:13.640
preserved so it passes through.

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:16.279
In terms of how does it actually
load balance the traffic.

00:04:16.279 --> 00:04:16.880
Right?

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:19.010
It can use either a two,
three, or five tuple

00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:20.348
hashing mechanism.

00:04:20.348 --> 00:04:21.890
So if you're using
a five tuple, it's

00:04:21.890 --> 00:04:26.430
based on source IP, dest
IP, source port, dest port,

00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:27.480
and protocol.

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:29.390
If you're using a
three tuple, it's

00:04:29.390 --> 00:04:31.690
source IP, dest
IP, and protocol.

00:04:31.690 --> 00:04:33.440
And if you're using
just a two tuple hash,

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:37.100
that's just basically
source IP and dest IP.

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:38.690
It also maintains
session affinity

00:04:38.690 --> 00:04:39.788
based on the IP address.

00:04:39.788 --> 00:04:41.330
So session affinity
is something that

00:04:41.330 --> 00:04:43.350
comes up a lot when we
talk about load balancers.

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:45.517
So just know, in terms of
the network load balancer,

00:04:45.517 --> 00:04:48.883
it's really basing
that on the IP address.

00:04:48.883 --> 00:04:50.300
It does support
HTTP health checks

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:51.930
in terms of the back ends.

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:53.430
And these are very
high performance,

00:04:53.430 --> 00:04:54.350
highly scalable devices.

00:04:54.350 --> 00:04:54.850
Right?

00:04:54.850 --> 00:04:56.975
They can handle a million
plus requests per second.

00:04:56.975 --> 00:04:59.308
So it's not something you
really have to worry about it,

00:04:59.308 --> 00:05:00.840
like how scalable
are these things?

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:01.520
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:03.562
I think you've touched on
a bunch of things here.

00:05:03.562 --> 00:05:07.100
You're able to still
enable that security

00:05:07.100 --> 00:05:09.290
by using the firewall
rules that you would

00:05:09.290 --> 00:05:10.760
apply to the VPC subnet itself.

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:11.510
RYAN PRZYBYL: Yep.

00:05:11.510 --> 00:05:13.135
STEPHANIE WONG: So
that doesn't change.

00:05:13.135 --> 00:05:14.847
And then also, this
is kind of the way

00:05:14.847 --> 00:05:16.930
that you're able to scale
your entire environment.

00:05:16.930 --> 00:05:17.420
RYAN PRZYBYL: Yep.

00:05:17.420 --> 00:05:17.945
Exactly.

00:05:17.945 --> 00:05:19.320
And because it's
a network layer,

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:21.080
it's very transparent in
terms of the traffic that's

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:22.510
coming in and getting
pushed to the back ends.

00:05:22.510 --> 00:05:22.790
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:05:22.790 --> 00:05:24.248
RYAN PRZYBYL: So
a lot of customers

00:05:24.248 --> 00:05:26.510
really like that function.

00:05:26.510 --> 00:05:29.650
So I'm going to move into
sort of our layer seven load

00:05:29.650 --> 00:05:30.440
balancers.

00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:33.370
So what we call our HTTP load
balancers or our Google Cloud

00:05:33.370 --> 00:05:35.792
load balancers.

00:05:35.792 --> 00:05:37.750
First, I want to talk
about DNS load balancing.

00:05:37.750 --> 00:05:39.875
This is a topic that comes
up a lot with customers,

00:05:39.875 --> 00:05:43.000
to say why don't we use DNS
load balancing, as far as how

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:44.920
our preferred mechanism,
our recommended way

00:05:44.920 --> 00:05:46.400
to solve this problem.

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:48.580
So the challenge with
DNS based load balancing

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:50.210
is kind of what I've
illustrated here.

00:05:50.210 --> 00:05:50.710
Right?

00:05:50.710 --> 00:05:52.377
So if you're running
multiple back ends,

00:05:52.377 --> 00:05:54.970
and they're running in various
regions, what you have to do

00:05:54.970 --> 00:05:57.370
is you have various DNS
records that are pointing

00:05:57.370 --> 00:05:58.760
to these various regions.

00:05:58.760 --> 00:05:59.740
There's a lot of
things in there that

00:05:59.740 --> 00:06:01.000
are outside of your control.

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:01.500
Right?

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:03.340
So, for example, you
could have a failure

00:06:03.340 --> 00:06:05.010
in, say, San Francisco.

00:06:05.010 --> 00:06:05.800
Right?

00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:07.667
You've got to update
the DNS potentially.

00:06:07.667 --> 00:06:09.750
You could have people that
have stale DNS entries.

00:06:09.750 --> 00:06:11.560
So even if they are
pounding away on this

00:06:11.560 --> 00:06:13.840
and it's not available, they
don't have the most current DNS

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:14.110
records.

00:06:14.110 --> 00:06:16.443
There's lots of things that
are outside of your control,

00:06:16.443 --> 00:06:17.110
per se.

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:18.010
Right?

00:06:18.010 --> 00:06:19.577
And when things
fail, guess what.

00:06:19.577 --> 00:06:21.410
You've got to go back
and update this stuff.

00:06:21.410 --> 00:06:23.110
So, for example, if
San Francisco fails,

00:06:23.110 --> 00:06:25.530
you have to make a change
so that you redirect all

00:06:25.530 --> 00:06:26.780
the traffic to another region.

00:06:26.780 --> 00:06:29.260
So it's not saying
that you can't use DNS

00:06:29.260 --> 00:06:30.610
based load balancing.

00:06:30.610 --> 00:06:32.860
It's just not the way that
Google has chosen to do it.

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:34.270
We looked at this
model and said,

00:06:34.270 --> 00:06:35.930
when you're running
something globally,

00:06:35.930 --> 00:06:37.430
how can we do this
that's different?

00:06:37.430 --> 00:06:38.847
How can you improve
on this model?

00:06:38.847 --> 00:06:40.337
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:06:40.337 --> 00:06:41.920
RYAN PRZYBYL: So
this is Google's sort

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:43.340
of answer to this.

00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:45.230
So this is our layer
seven load balancer.

00:06:45.230 --> 00:06:47.020
And this is a really
powerful construct.

00:06:47.020 --> 00:06:47.650
Right?

00:06:47.650 --> 00:06:49.660
I always like to tell customers,
this is like an Indy car

00:06:49.660 --> 00:06:52.000
that Google built that we
just gave them the keys for.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:54.250
The reason I say that is
because this is the same load

00:06:54.250 --> 00:06:56.860
balancer that we use
for Gmail, for YouTube,

00:06:56.860 --> 00:06:58.010
for our search engine.

00:06:58.010 --> 00:07:00.340
We didn't build this as a
product to say, hey, Cloud.

00:07:00.340 --> 00:07:00.940
Go sell this.

00:07:00.940 --> 00:07:03.220
We built this because
we needed to just

00:07:03.220 --> 00:07:04.550
to make Google function.

00:07:04.550 --> 00:07:06.160
So really it is this
Indy car that we

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:08.590
built in Google infrastructure
that we've said, hey, go take

00:07:08.590 --> 00:07:09.640
it for a spin around the track.

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:09.810
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:07:09.810 --> 00:07:11.185
I was about to
ask whether or not

00:07:11.185 --> 00:07:13.210
this is built on the
original infrastructure

00:07:13.210 --> 00:07:16.570
that we're using previously for
our billion plus user products.

00:07:16.570 --> 00:07:17.658
RYAN PRZYBYL: Exactly.

00:07:17.658 --> 00:07:19.075
So the way this
works, and the way

00:07:19.075 --> 00:07:21.070
this is very different
is to start off,

00:07:21.070 --> 00:07:22.670
you have one VIP globally.

00:07:22.670 --> 00:07:24.740
So in this case, if
you're running MyApp.com

00:07:24.740 --> 00:07:26.157
in that regional
construct, you're

00:07:26.157 --> 00:07:28.240
at a different IP
for every region.

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:31.280
In this case, you're using
200.1.1.1 in this case.

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:31.780
Right?

00:07:31.780 --> 00:07:33.535
So whether you're in
Asia, whether you're

00:07:33.535 --> 00:07:34.570
in Europe, whether
you're in America,

00:07:34.570 --> 00:07:36.430
you're always
hitting that same IP.

00:07:36.430 --> 00:07:37.210
Right?

00:07:37.210 --> 00:07:39.010
So what happens is
when you come in,

00:07:39.010 --> 00:07:41.800
we're advertising a
global set of blocks.

00:07:41.800 --> 00:07:45.100
So the various ISPs, the
way that you get to Google,

00:07:45.100 --> 00:07:46.780
or when I say you,
the way customers

00:07:46.780 --> 00:07:49.570
get to Google to sort of access
your MyApp.com is they're

00:07:49.570 --> 00:07:51.640
going to come in from
the internet somewhere.

00:07:51.640 --> 00:07:53.370
So it's going to hit one
of our load balancers.

00:07:53.370 --> 00:07:54.400
And our load
balancer is actually

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:56.108
going to look at all
the back ends you've

00:07:56.108 --> 00:07:57.250
configured on the backside.

00:07:57.250 --> 00:07:58.792
And it's actually
going to figure out

00:07:58.792 --> 00:08:00.182
what is the closest one to it?

00:08:00.182 --> 00:08:02.140
So maybe you're running
one on east west coast,

00:08:02.140 --> 00:08:03.890
and you have somebody
come in from Europe.

00:08:03.890 --> 00:08:06.400
It's going to push it to the
west or to the east coast.

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:07.960
If you're running
it in all regions,

00:08:07.960 --> 00:08:09.070
then if it comes
in in Europe, it's

00:08:09.070 --> 00:08:10.195
going to land it in Europe.

00:08:10.195 --> 00:08:12.730
So net, net it gives your
customer better performance,

00:08:12.730 --> 00:08:14.920
because that actually
get routed to the back

00:08:14.920 --> 00:08:16.330
ends that are closest to them.

00:08:16.330 --> 00:08:17.305
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:08:17.305 --> 00:08:19.228
And this kind of reminds
me of CDN as well.

00:08:19.228 --> 00:08:20.020
RYAN PRZYBYL: Yeah.

00:08:20.020 --> 00:08:20.770
It's very similar.

00:08:20.770 --> 00:08:22.913
So our CDN actually kind
of lays parallel to this.

00:08:22.913 --> 00:08:24.580
When you have CDN
content, this is going

00:08:24.580 --> 00:08:25.480
to be the first thing you hit.

00:08:25.480 --> 00:08:27.063
And then it redirects
it over to a CDN

00:08:27.063 --> 00:08:29.090
to serve what the
customers are asking for.

00:08:29.090 --> 00:08:31.920
But this is sort of in
concept how it works.

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:33.950
You can see Maya, Bob,
and Shen in this example,

00:08:33.950 --> 00:08:35.950
they're all hitting the
same IP, and they're all

00:08:35.950 --> 00:08:39.183
getting pushed to serving back
ends in their various regions.

00:08:39.183 --> 00:08:41.350
Now, this is probably one
of our most popular things

00:08:41.350 --> 00:08:43.330
that we sell in terms
of load balancing,

00:08:43.330 --> 00:08:44.735
because it just is so powerful.

00:08:44.735 --> 00:08:47.110
Lots of customers, even if
they're running their own load

00:08:47.110 --> 00:08:49.030
balancer stacks love
the fact that we

00:08:49.030 --> 00:08:52.197
can have an AnyCast VIP globally
that they can leverage here.

00:08:52.197 --> 00:08:54.280
So they might stack this
load balancer on the edge

00:08:54.280 --> 00:08:55.840
and then put their load
balancers behind it.

00:08:55.840 --> 00:08:57.000
STEPHANIE WONG: I see.

00:08:57.000 --> 00:08:58.010
RYAN PRZYBYL: When
we talk about DDOS,

00:08:58.010 --> 00:08:59.140
when we talk about all
sorts of other stuff,

00:08:59.140 --> 00:09:00.370
this is what sits at our edge.

00:09:00.370 --> 00:09:02.662
This is what helps protect
against a lot of that stuff.

00:09:02.662 --> 00:09:04.510
So, again, it's a very
powerful construct.

00:09:04.510 --> 00:09:06.260
STEPHANIE WONG: And
just to reiterate, you

00:09:06.260 --> 00:09:07.900
mentioned a single AnyCast IP.

00:09:07.900 --> 00:09:11.230
Why is that so beneficial and
such a differentiator for us?

00:09:11.230 --> 00:09:12.730
RYAN PRZYBYL: So
the big thing there

00:09:12.730 --> 00:09:14.980
is you don't have to deal
with the DNS piece of it.

00:09:14.980 --> 00:09:15.480
Right?

00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:18.220
So in the case I showed you
before, you have an IP address

00:09:18.220 --> 00:09:19.840
for one region, an IP
address for another region.

00:09:19.840 --> 00:09:20.770
An IP address for
another region.

00:09:20.770 --> 00:09:23.090
If a region fails, you
have to update DNS records.

00:09:23.090 --> 00:09:24.860
You have to make sure those
DNS records get propagated out.

00:09:24.860 --> 00:09:25.300
Right?

00:09:25.300 --> 00:09:25.720
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:09:25.720 --> 00:09:27.810
RYAN PRZYBYL: In this
case, you only have one IP.

00:09:27.810 --> 00:09:28.310
Right?

00:09:28.310 --> 00:09:31.865
So you never have to do any sort
of updating to the DNS records

00:09:31.865 --> 00:09:32.740
for this to function.

00:09:32.740 --> 00:09:35.710
You're always hitting,
in this case, 200.1.1.1.

00:09:35.710 --> 00:09:36.260
Right?

00:09:36.260 --> 00:09:37.420
So no matter where
you are in the world,

00:09:37.420 --> 00:09:38.878
you're always
hitting that same IP.

00:09:38.878 --> 00:09:41.368
And you're letting Google
handle what region to push it

00:09:41.368 --> 00:09:43.660
to that's closest to your
customer to give you the best

00:09:43.660 --> 00:09:44.320
customer experience.

00:09:44.320 --> 00:09:44.680
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:09:44.680 --> 00:09:46.457
In terms of our load
balancer, and you've

00:09:46.457 --> 00:09:49.040
mentioned this because we have
regional load balancer as well,

00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:51.250
but we have high
availability built into it.

00:09:51.250 --> 00:09:51.700
RYAN PRZYBYL: Exactly.

00:09:51.700 --> 00:09:53.770
You can think of these as
many, many processes that

00:09:53.770 --> 00:09:54.850
are running on Google's edge.

00:09:54.850 --> 00:09:55.350
Right?

00:09:55.350 --> 00:09:58.700
So as part of how we manage DDOS
and how we sort of handle that,

00:09:58.700 --> 00:10:00.200
you can think of
these as a process.

00:10:00.200 --> 00:10:02.140
So when we need more of
these, what do we do?

00:10:02.140 --> 00:10:03.848
Well, we spin up more
of these processes.

00:10:03.848 --> 00:10:05.440
So it's, in a way,
I don't want to say

00:10:05.440 --> 00:10:07.600
infinitely scalable, because
nothing is infinitely scalable.

00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:07.870
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:10:07.870 --> 00:10:09.280
RYAN PRZYBYL: But it's
very highly scalable,

00:10:09.280 --> 00:10:11.405
because as we need more,
we instantaneously spin up

00:10:11.405 --> 00:10:13.840
more of these load balancers
to handle the incoming load.

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:13.920
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:15.760
And this is all based
on our Google front end.

00:10:15.760 --> 00:10:16.980
And that's kind of the
technology of that.

00:10:16.980 --> 00:10:17.150
RYAN PRZYBYL: Yep.

00:10:17.150 --> 00:10:19.108
And sometimes you hear
us use the term for GFE.

00:10:19.108 --> 00:10:20.400
It stands for Google Front End.

00:10:20.400 --> 00:10:22.790
That's our internal terminology
for our global cloud load

00:10:22.790 --> 00:10:23.290
balancer.

00:10:23.290 --> 00:10:24.123
STEPHANIE WONG: Yes.

00:10:25.502 --> 00:10:27.960
RYAN PRZYBYL: So I want to get
into sort of the data model.

00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:29.320
How do you actually
build these things?

00:10:29.320 --> 00:10:29.550
Right?

00:10:29.550 --> 00:10:30.425
How do they function?

00:10:30.425 --> 00:10:32.400
So on the left hand side
you have the internet.

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:32.560
Right?

00:10:32.560 --> 00:10:34.160
This is where all your
customers are coming in.

00:10:34.160 --> 00:10:36.470
The first thing, it's going to
be a global forwarding rule.

00:10:36.470 --> 00:10:36.750
Right?

00:10:36.750 --> 00:10:37.980
How are you going
to forward these.

00:10:37.980 --> 00:10:38.480
Right?

00:10:38.480 --> 00:10:41.980
So www.stephanie.com might
forward to one back end.

00:10:41.980 --> 00:10:45.972
And
www.stephanie.com/buymycoolstuff

00:10:45.972 --> 00:10:47.555
might forward to a
different back end.

00:10:47.555 --> 00:10:47.850
Right?

00:10:47.850 --> 00:10:49.320
So when we talk about
those forwarding rules,

00:10:49.320 --> 00:10:50.695
this is what we're
talking about.

00:10:50.695 --> 00:10:52.590
How do you forward
based on the URLs?

00:10:52.590 --> 00:10:53.278
OK?

00:10:53.278 --> 00:10:55.320
This is going to direct
then to that target proxy

00:10:55.320 --> 00:10:56.990
where you actually
build the URL maps.

00:10:56.990 --> 00:10:57.180
Right?

00:10:57.180 --> 00:10:58.000
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:10:58.000 --> 00:10:59.970
RYAN PRZYBYL: And then those
map to back end services.

00:10:59.970 --> 00:11:00.470
Right?

00:11:00.470 --> 00:11:02.350
So it could be a web page.

00:11:02.350 --> 00:11:03.720
It could be a shopping cart.

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:06.010
It could be anything that
you put on on the internet.

00:11:06.010 --> 00:11:06.510
Right?

00:11:06.510 --> 00:11:08.485
So that is your
back end service.

00:11:08.485 --> 00:11:10.110
And then those back
end services really

00:11:10.110 --> 00:11:12.330
live in what we call
managed instance groups.

00:11:12.330 --> 00:11:12.880
Right?

00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:15.213
So I'll talk a bit more about
those when I set those up.

00:11:15.213 --> 00:11:18.010
But effectively you can
consider those a scalable,

00:11:18.010 --> 00:11:20.980
highly available way of
running that sort of back end.

00:11:20.980 --> 00:11:21.480
Right?

00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:23.397
So whether that be that
web server that you're

00:11:23.397 --> 00:11:25.387
trying to get to, or
that shopping application

00:11:25.387 --> 00:11:26.220
that you're running.

00:11:26.220 --> 00:11:26.670
Right?

00:11:26.670 --> 00:11:29.253
They're really running in that
on top of that managed instance

00:11:29.253 --> 00:11:30.295
group.

00:11:30.295 --> 00:11:32.670
And as we talked about earlier,
we've got firewall rules.

00:11:32.670 --> 00:11:35.170
Firewall rules are in place in
here to help control traffic.

00:11:35.170 --> 00:11:36.750
Now, in this case you're not
using the firewall rules.

00:11:36.750 --> 00:11:36.920
OK.

00:11:36.920 --> 00:11:38.550
We're at the network
load balancer.

00:11:38.550 --> 00:11:41.555
Because the client IP,
as I mentioned before,

00:11:41.555 --> 00:11:42.430
isn't passed through.

00:11:42.430 --> 00:11:44.910
The client actually terminates
at our load balancer.

00:11:44.910 --> 00:11:46.110
So it does SSL termination.

00:11:46.110 --> 00:11:46.943
It does other stuff.

00:11:46.943 --> 00:11:48.750
It terminates the
session there, opens

00:11:48.750 --> 00:11:50.020
a new session on the backside.

00:11:50.020 --> 00:11:50.520
Right?

00:11:50.520 --> 00:11:53.240
So everything is
private IPs inside.

00:11:53.240 --> 00:11:54.870
The only public
facing IPs are really

00:11:54.870 --> 00:11:56.260
what's facing out
to the internet

00:11:56.260 --> 00:11:57.560
and what the customers
are talking to.

00:11:57.560 --> 00:11:57.780
Right?

00:11:57.780 --> 00:11:58.860
In your end customers.

00:11:58.860 --> 00:12:01.410
Not you in particular.

00:12:01.410 --> 00:12:04.000
STEPHANIE WONG: And just to go
back to that for just a second.

00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:05.970
You just mentioned internal IPs.

00:12:05.970 --> 00:12:08.705
And that reminds me of what
you're about to talk about,

00:12:08.705 --> 00:12:10.080
which is internal
load balancing.

00:12:10.080 --> 00:12:12.760
At what stage does it kind
of become from external

00:12:12.760 --> 00:12:13.260
to internal?

00:12:13.260 --> 00:12:14.280
RYAN PRZYBYL: A good
way to think about

00:12:14.280 --> 00:12:15.613
that, it's a very good question.

00:12:15.613 --> 00:12:17.390
So let's take a shopping
cart application.

00:12:17.390 --> 00:12:17.970
Right?

00:12:17.970 --> 00:12:19.625
So your shopping
cart application

00:12:19.625 --> 00:12:21.000
may be published
on the internet.

00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:22.980
So you put that behind
the load balancer.

00:12:22.980 --> 00:12:25.590
And the load balancer is the
public IP for your application.

00:12:25.590 --> 00:12:27.090
Customers come in
from the internet.

00:12:27.090 --> 00:12:28.173
They hit that application.

00:12:28.173 --> 00:12:28.800
Right?

00:12:28.800 --> 00:12:30.960
Now that application
has to access inventory.

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:33.333
It has to access a shopping
cart sort of back end.

00:12:33.333 --> 00:12:35.500
It has to access all these
other things to function.

00:12:35.500 --> 00:12:36.330
OK?

00:12:36.330 --> 00:12:37.933
It will use the
internal load balancer

00:12:37.933 --> 00:12:40.350
to then dovetail into all those
other applications running

00:12:40.350 --> 00:12:41.017
on the backside.

00:12:41.017 --> 00:12:43.020
So you're using the
global ILB upfront

00:12:43.020 --> 00:12:45.853
to access that web page
or that shopping cart.

00:12:45.853 --> 00:12:48.270
And the shopping cart is using
the internal load balancers

00:12:48.270 --> 00:12:50.270
to access all those other
things on the back end

00:12:50.270 --> 00:12:51.420
that it needs to function.

00:12:51.420 --> 00:12:54.012
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:12:54.012 --> 00:12:54.720
RYAN PRZYBYL: OK.

00:12:54.720 --> 00:12:57.300
So let's run through some of
the features that are really

00:12:57.300 --> 00:12:59.700
tied to our layer
seven load balancer.

00:12:59.700 --> 00:13:01.780
So we talked about the
single global AnyCast VIP.

00:13:01.780 --> 00:13:02.280
Right?

00:13:02.280 --> 00:13:04.720
And this can be IPv4 or IPv6.

00:13:04.720 --> 00:13:05.220
Right?

00:13:05.220 --> 00:13:08.190
And it runs globally.

00:13:08.190 --> 00:13:09.090
Worldwide capacity.

00:13:09.090 --> 00:13:09.750
We talked about this.

00:13:09.750 --> 00:13:09.900
Right?

00:13:09.900 --> 00:13:11.983
These are processes that
run out at Google's edge.

00:13:11.983 --> 00:13:13.130
They're highly scalable.

00:13:13.130 --> 00:13:15.530
And like I said, we use these
for things like our search

00:13:15.530 --> 00:13:16.030
engine.

00:13:16.030 --> 00:13:17.160
So we know they're tried.

00:13:17.160 --> 00:13:18.150
They're tested.

00:13:18.150 --> 00:13:20.160
They run really well.

00:13:20.160 --> 00:13:21.618
Cross region failover
and failback.

00:13:21.618 --> 00:13:23.785
So this is kind of what I
was talking about earlier.

00:13:23.785 --> 00:13:26.050
You might have instances
running at all regions.

00:13:26.050 --> 00:13:28.878
But if, say, something
fails in Europe, guess what.

00:13:28.878 --> 00:13:30.420
The load balancer
knows Europe is not

00:13:30.420 --> 00:13:31.590
in commission at the moment.

00:13:31.590 --> 00:13:34.110
I'm going to fail that to,
say, the east coast of the US.

00:13:34.110 --> 00:13:35.652
Cause that's the
next closest region.

00:13:35.652 --> 00:13:38.350
Or if you're in the west
coast of the US and it fails,

00:13:38.350 --> 00:13:40.350
it's going to say, oh, I
know my next closest is

00:13:40.350 --> 00:13:41.142
the central region.

00:13:41.142 --> 00:13:44.600
So all that intelligence
lives with the load balancer.

00:13:44.600 --> 00:13:47.652
And it figures out, how do I
get it to the closest back end

00:13:47.652 --> 00:13:49.860
to ultimately give your
customer the best experience?

00:13:49.860 --> 00:13:50.900
STEPHANIE WONG: Awesome.

00:13:50.900 --> 00:13:52.650
RYAN PRZYBYL: We talked
about auto scaling

00:13:52.650 --> 00:13:54.233
in terms of how we
use these with DDOS

00:13:54.233 --> 00:13:57.750
and various other things
so it's incredibly quick.

00:13:57.750 --> 00:14:00.100
It's also a single point to
apply Googleable policies.

00:14:00.100 --> 00:14:00.600
Right?

00:14:00.600 --> 00:14:03.080
So we talked about URLs, various
sort of security policies

00:14:03.080 --> 00:14:04.080
you can overlay on that.

00:14:04.080 --> 00:14:05.520
We're not going to talk
about Cloud Armor here.

00:14:05.520 --> 00:14:07.687
But Cloud Armor sort of
works in function with this,

00:14:07.687 --> 00:14:10.230
if you want to whitelist
or blacklist IP blocks.

00:14:10.230 --> 00:14:12.060
All of that stuff
works in conjunction

00:14:12.060 --> 00:14:14.210
over the top of these
layer seven load balancers.

00:14:14.210 --> 00:14:15.120
STEPHANIE WONG: OK.

00:14:15.120 --> 00:14:16.920
RYAN PRZYBYL: And
again, super robust.

00:14:16.920 --> 00:14:18.420
Millions of queries per second.

00:14:18.420 --> 00:14:19.150
Right?

00:14:19.150 --> 00:14:20.490
It wouldn't function
for our search engine

00:14:20.490 --> 00:14:22.615
if we didn't have that sort
of capability in there.

00:14:22.615 --> 00:14:24.762
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:14:24.762 --> 00:14:25.470
RYAN PRZYBYL: OK.

00:14:25.470 --> 00:14:28.030
So, as you said, I'm going
to pivot to the internal load

00:14:28.030 --> 00:14:28.530
balancers.

00:14:28.530 --> 00:14:28.690
Right?

00:14:28.690 --> 00:14:30.898
So these are things that
are not facing the internet.

00:14:30.898 --> 00:14:32.280
They're not facing externally.

00:14:32.280 --> 00:14:35.070
These are facing, typically
you can think of it like VM

00:14:35.070 --> 00:14:38.138
to VM inside your
cloud environment.

00:14:38.138 --> 00:14:39.930
So one of the key things
to understand here

00:14:39.930 --> 00:14:42.220
is there really is
no load balancer.

00:14:42.220 --> 00:14:42.720
Right?

00:14:42.720 --> 00:14:45.213
When you go and configure
a load balancer,

00:14:45.213 --> 00:14:47.130
you're actually configuring
the control plane.

00:14:47.130 --> 00:14:47.630
Right?

00:14:47.630 --> 00:14:48.720
Our SDN control plane.

00:14:48.720 --> 00:14:50.520
It's not like you're
building a box that's

00:14:50.520 --> 00:14:51.810
running on top of a VM.

00:14:51.810 --> 00:14:52.447
Right?

00:14:52.447 --> 00:14:54.030
Because that will
create a bottleneck.

00:14:54.030 --> 00:14:54.600
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:14:54.600 --> 00:14:56.220
RYAN PRZYBYL: So by
basically allowing

00:14:56.220 --> 00:14:58.733
you to program the SDN
directly, when packets come in

00:14:58.733 --> 00:15:00.150
and they need to
be load balanced,

00:15:00.150 --> 00:15:01.750
the SDN just sort of
looks at them and goes,

00:15:01.750 --> 00:15:03.750
oh, I know that there's
four back ends for this.

00:15:03.750 --> 00:15:05.130
I'm going to pick one.

00:15:05.130 --> 00:15:05.920
I'm going to send
it to the back end.

00:15:05.920 --> 00:15:06.030
Right?

00:15:06.030 --> 00:15:07.740
It doesn't actually forward
it to a load balancer.

00:15:07.740 --> 00:15:09.600
And a load balancer doesn't
make a load balancing decision

00:15:09.600 --> 00:15:11.183
and then forward it
to one of the VMs.

00:15:11.183 --> 00:15:11.730
Right?

00:15:11.730 --> 00:15:14.103
So why we chose to
do that is, again, we

00:15:14.103 --> 00:15:16.020
don't want a single point
of failure in there.

00:15:16.020 --> 00:15:17.640
We don't want something
that can fail.

00:15:17.640 --> 00:15:19.320
When we start talking about
the SDN and the control plane,

00:15:19.320 --> 00:15:22.280
if it's failing we have larger
issues that we're dealing with.

00:15:22.280 --> 00:15:23.197
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:15:25.645 --> 00:15:27.270
RYAN PRZYBYL: So the
data model on this

00:15:27.270 --> 00:15:28.350
looks a little bit different.

00:15:28.350 --> 00:15:28.590
Right?

00:15:28.590 --> 00:15:30.360
So in this case, you
have a client VM.

00:15:30.360 --> 00:15:33.030
So like I said, your
shopping cart application.

00:15:33.030 --> 00:15:33.970
You have IP addresses.

00:15:33.970 --> 00:15:34.470
Right?

00:15:34.470 --> 00:15:35.220
So back ends.

00:15:35.220 --> 00:15:36.780
What VMs are they?

00:15:36.780 --> 00:15:37.320
What IPs?

00:15:37.320 --> 00:15:39.790
What ports and protocols
are you using on them?

00:15:39.790 --> 00:15:41.680
The serving capacity
and the instance health,

00:15:41.680 --> 00:15:42.880
we'll talk about
managed instance groups

00:15:42.880 --> 00:15:45.280
when I show you how to actually
configure these things.

00:15:45.280 --> 00:15:47.700
But effectively, that
managed instance group

00:15:47.700 --> 00:15:49.020
is going to be health checked.

00:15:49.020 --> 00:15:49.270
Right?

00:15:49.270 --> 00:15:51.020
So what do I mean when
I say health check?

00:15:51.020 --> 00:15:52.620
Like we're actually
probing it to say,

00:15:52.620 --> 00:15:55.410
is the instance or
instances healthy?

00:15:55.410 --> 00:15:58.740
If the instance in the
group is not healthy,

00:15:58.740 --> 00:15:59.740
let's auto heal it.

00:15:59.740 --> 00:16:00.240
Right?

00:16:00.240 --> 00:16:01.657
So we'll actually
spin it back up.

00:16:01.657 --> 00:16:03.900
And if, let's say, you're
running two, and one fails,

00:16:03.900 --> 00:16:04.890
we'll spin up another one.

00:16:04.890 --> 00:16:06.307
And now you've got
your two again.

00:16:06.307 --> 00:16:08.280
It also allows you to
dynamically scale these.

00:16:08.280 --> 00:16:08.780
Right?

00:16:08.780 --> 00:16:10.320
So if you think
about, let's say,

00:16:10.320 --> 00:16:11.910
Black Friday is a
really good example.

00:16:11.910 --> 00:16:12.180
Right?

00:16:12.180 --> 00:16:13.890
I'm running a whole
bunch of back ends.

00:16:13.890 --> 00:16:16.260
And I get a whole bunch of
customer traffic hitting

00:16:16.260 --> 00:16:17.400
my shopping app.

00:16:17.400 --> 00:16:19.087
Well, you need more back ends?

00:16:19.087 --> 00:16:20.670
You can set the
managed instance group

00:16:20.670 --> 00:16:24.445
to scale this stuff horizontally
to build more back ends for you

00:16:24.445 --> 00:16:27.180
dynamically to allow you
to scale that traffic up.

00:16:27.180 --> 00:16:29.960
The great thing about it is,
it dynamically scales up.

00:16:29.960 --> 00:16:31.710
When the traffic goes
away, it dynamically

00:16:31.710 --> 00:16:32.660
will scale that back down.

00:16:32.660 --> 00:16:33.060
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:16:33.060 --> 00:16:34.320
RYAN PRZYBYL: So you
set all that stuff up.

00:16:34.320 --> 00:16:34.950
STEPHANIE WONG:
That's incredible.

00:16:34.950 --> 00:16:37.440
Because, again, if you can
scale down to one, let's

00:16:37.440 --> 00:16:40.320
say, at minimum, then you
are achieving a lot more cost

00:16:40.320 --> 00:16:41.070
savings that way.

00:16:41.070 --> 00:16:42.120
RYAN PRZYBYL: Yep.

00:16:42.120 --> 00:16:42.780
It's a really good point.

00:16:42.780 --> 00:16:44.040
I always recommend running two.

00:16:44.040 --> 00:16:45.960
So if one fails, there's
always one available.

00:16:45.960 --> 00:16:47.310
So I kind of consider
two the minimum.

00:16:47.310 --> 00:16:47.790
But yeah, you're right.

00:16:47.790 --> 00:16:49.170
You can actually set up
just one if you want to.

00:16:49.170 --> 00:16:50.045
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:16:50.045 --> 00:16:52.230
And here it's like, these
are homogeneous VMs.

00:16:52.230 --> 00:16:53.730
Like let's say an app server.

00:16:53.730 --> 00:16:56.010
And it gives you, A,
failover capabilities,

00:16:56.010 --> 00:16:58.500
and the ability to auto
scale based on health checks,

00:16:58.500 --> 00:17:00.190
or whatever configuration
that you set.

00:17:00.190 --> 00:17:00.690
Right?

00:17:00.690 --> 00:17:03.162
Based on VM utilization or
CPU utilization, for example.

00:17:03.162 --> 00:17:04.079
RYAN PRZYBYL: Exactly.

00:17:07.099 --> 00:17:08.810
So now we're going
to talk about some

00:17:08.810 --> 00:17:10.917
of the features for these
network load balancers.

00:17:10.917 --> 00:17:12.500
These are the internal
load balancers.

00:17:12.500 --> 00:17:13.130
Right?

00:17:13.130 --> 00:17:15.800
So just like the external load
balancers, they work on a VIP.

00:17:15.800 --> 00:17:16.000
Right?

00:17:16.000 --> 00:17:17.700
So there's going to be an
IP address assigned to them.

00:17:17.700 --> 00:17:19.819
So when traffic is coming
from your front end,

00:17:19.819 --> 00:17:22.099
like your shopping cart app,
and it's going to the back ends,

00:17:22.099 --> 00:17:23.150
it's going to go to a VIP.

00:17:23.150 --> 00:17:23.650
Right?

00:17:23.650 --> 00:17:26.089
So you may have five VMs
that are the back ends,

00:17:26.089 --> 00:17:28.550
but they're all nested
behind that single VIP.

00:17:28.550 --> 00:17:29.120
Right?

00:17:29.120 --> 00:17:30.912
So what really happens
is the SDN will say,

00:17:30.912 --> 00:17:33.540
oh, I know that VIP is
configured as a load balancer.

00:17:33.540 --> 00:17:35.290
I know these five VMs
are on the backside,

00:17:35.290 --> 00:17:37.120
and it's going to pick
one of them, send it.

00:17:37.120 --> 00:17:38.370
The VM is going to receive it.

00:17:38.370 --> 00:17:40.810
It's going to see, oh, this
isn't my IP address per se,

00:17:40.810 --> 00:17:43.310
but it's a VIP that I know that
I'm the back end from a load

00:17:43.310 --> 00:17:43.810
balancer.

00:17:43.810 --> 00:17:45.407
So I can receive that packet.

00:17:45.407 --> 00:17:46.490
I can process that packet.

00:17:46.490 --> 00:17:48.748
And I can respond
to that packet.

00:17:48.748 --> 00:17:50.540
We talked a little bit
about health checks,

00:17:50.540 --> 00:17:53.180
TCP, HTTP, HTTPS health checks.

00:17:53.180 --> 00:17:55.760
This is really sort
of what it uses

00:17:55.760 --> 00:17:58.550
to check and make sure
those back ends are healthy.

00:17:58.550 --> 00:18:00.390
In this case, the
client IP is preserved.

00:18:00.390 --> 00:18:01.390
So we talked about that.

00:18:01.390 --> 00:18:03.960
Because you're dealing with
VM to VM here in most cases.

00:18:03.960 --> 00:18:04.460
Right?

00:18:04.460 --> 00:18:07.463
So we're maintaining
VM A's IP address when

00:18:07.463 --> 00:18:08.630
you send to those back ends.

00:18:08.630 --> 00:18:08.880
Right?

00:18:08.880 --> 00:18:08.960
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:18:08.960 --> 00:18:09.320
RYAN PRZYBYL: So
that's really what

00:18:09.320 --> 00:18:11.580
we're saying when we say
the client IP is preserved.

00:18:11.580 --> 00:18:14.750
It is really the VM IP.

00:18:14.750 --> 00:18:17.570
And we talked about there's
really no middle proxy.

00:18:17.570 --> 00:18:19.500
There's no actual sort
of choke point in this.

00:18:19.500 --> 00:18:20.000
Right?

00:18:20.000 --> 00:18:21.860
Because you are configuring
the SDN directly.

00:18:21.860 --> 00:18:22.360
Right?

00:18:22.360 --> 00:18:25.045
So that's how we deliver super
highly scalable capacity here

00:18:25.045 --> 00:18:27.170
to do this, where there is
no sort of choke points,

00:18:27.170 --> 00:18:28.253
or things that can break--

00:18:28.253 --> 00:18:29.128
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:18:29.128 --> 00:18:30.000
It's not a server.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:30.400
It's not a VM.

00:18:30.400 --> 00:18:31.560
It's not a physical device.

00:18:31.560 --> 00:18:32.030
RYAN PRZYBYL: Yeah.

00:18:32.030 --> 00:18:34.280
If you've configured F5
load balancers or something

00:18:34.280 --> 00:18:35.690
in your data center,
it's a physical device

00:18:35.690 --> 00:18:36.410
that you put in there.

00:18:36.410 --> 00:18:36.476
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:18:36.476 --> 00:18:36.510
Exactly.

00:18:36.510 --> 00:18:37.100
RYAN PRZYBYL: In
this case, there's

00:18:37.100 --> 00:18:38.090
nothing physical about it.

00:18:38.090 --> 00:18:39.680
It's just a control
plane configuration.

00:18:39.680 --> 00:18:40.130
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:18:40.130 --> 00:18:40.310
RYAN PRZYBYL: Right.

00:18:40.310 --> 00:18:42.000
So it's very elegant
in its simplicity.

00:18:42.000 --> 00:18:43.310
STEPHANIE WONG: It's a different
way of thinking, honestly,

00:18:43.310 --> 00:18:44.450
with the SDN.

00:18:44.450 --> 00:18:44.630
RYAN PRZYBYL: Yeah.

00:18:44.630 --> 00:18:46.880
There's a lot of stuff that
Google does that really we

00:18:46.880 --> 00:18:49.002
embody this theory
of why make it

00:18:49.002 --> 00:18:50.210
more complex than we have to?

00:18:50.210 --> 00:18:54.330
If we can simplify, and make
it more elegant and simple,

00:18:54.330 --> 00:18:55.240
let's go.

00:18:55.240 --> 00:18:55.740
Let's do it.

00:18:55.740 --> 00:18:56.615
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:18:56.615 --> 00:18:58.352
Absolutely.

00:18:58.352 --> 00:18:59.060
RYAN PRZYBYL: OK.

00:18:59.060 --> 00:19:01.920
So that's my high
level overview of this.

00:19:01.920 --> 00:19:04.820
Now I'm actually going to take
you into configuring a load

00:19:04.820 --> 00:19:05.870
balancer.

00:19:05.870 --> 00:19:10.430
So since our global load
balancers, the layer seven

00:19:10.430 --> 00:19:12.230
ones, the Indy
car Ferraris I was

00:19:12.230 --> 00:19:13.775
sort of talking
about are probably

00:19:13.775 --> 00:19:16.177
a very common used
application, I'm

00:19:16.177 --> 00:19:18.510
going to take you through how
to configure one of those.

00:19:18.510 --> 00:19:21.350
So if you have ever
been in our console,

00:19:21.350 --> 00:19:24.570
you'll recognize this as sort
of the home page of the console.

00:19:24.570 --> 00:19:26.780
So in configuring a
global load balancer,

00:19:26.780 --> 00:19:28.730
you have to actually do
a few things before I

00:19:28.730 --> 00:19:29.600
configure the load balancer.

00:19:29.600 --> 00:19:31.320
We talked about managed
instance groups.

00:19:31.320 --> 00:19:31.820
Right?

00:19:31.820 --> 00:19:33.440
I didn't really touch
on them super in-depth.

00:19:33.440 --> 00:19:34.865
But I'm going to show you
how to actually configure

00:19:34.865 --> 00:19:36.170
a managed instance group.

00:19:36.170 --> 00:19:38.990
Because that's what you use to
put behind the load balancer,

00:19:38.990 --> 00:19:40.930
or what I used in my case.

00:19:40.930 --> 00:19:42.710
OK?

00:19:42.710 --> 00:19:45.230
So let's go and look at that.

00:19:45.230 --> 00:19:47.198
So when you click
on Compute Engine,

00:19:47.198 --> 00:19:49.490
you're going to instance
groups and instance templates.

00:19:49.490 --> 00:19:51.830
Before you can build a
managed instance group,

00:19:51.830 --> 00:19:53.720
you have to have a
template for the VM

00:19:53.720 --> 00:19:55.053
that's going to be in the group.

00:19:55.053 --> 00:19:58.550
Because the group basically will
spawn copies of that particular

00:19:58.550 --> 00:19:59.520
VM that you specify.

00:19:59.520 --> 00:20:00.020
Right?

00:20:00.020 --> 00:20:02.480
So it's not like you can have
five different types of VMs.

00:20:02.480 --> 00:20:02.780
Right?

00:20:02.780 --> 00:20:05.270
If you say VM A looks like
this, the managed instance group

00:20:05.270 --> 00:20:06.920
might be five VM As.

00:20:06.920 --> 00:20:07.670
Right?

00:20:07.670 --> 00:20:11.470
The instant template is going to
find what does VM A look like.

00:20:11.470 --> 00:20:12.110
OK?

00:20:12.110 --> 00:20:14.120
So that's the first
place we're going to go.

00:20:14.120 --> 00:20:16.522
Now, I've pre-built one,
because it takes some time

00:20:16.522 --> 00:20:18.230
to update these things,
specifically when

00:20:18.230 --> 00:20:20.350
you go and do the actual
load balancing configuration,

00:20:20.350 --> 00:20:21.950
cause like I said, we have
thousands of these processes

00:20:21.950 --> 00:20:22.880
running out there.

00:20:22.880 --> 00:20:23.750
And you could
imagine how long it

00:20:23.750 --> 00:20:26.210
takes to push all these new
configs out to all this stuff.

00:20:26.210 --> 00:20:26.710
Right?

00:20:26.710 --> 00:20:28.903
So I intentionally
pre-configured this stuff

00:20:28.903 --> 00:20:31.070
so you wouldn't have to
wait for me for five minutes

00:20:31.070 --> 00:20:32.360
and watch the little
thing spin around.

00:20:32.360 --> 00:20:32.630
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:20:32.630 --> 00:20:33.240
RYAN PRZYBYL: But I'm
going to show you how

00:20:33.240 --> 00:20:34.532
to create an instance template.

00:20:34.532 --> 00:20:37.320
So here we go.

00:20:37.320 --> 00:20:38.750
So this is an instance template.

00:20:38.750 --> 00:20:41.370
And as I said, you're basically
configuring a VM here.

00:20:41.370 --> 00:20:41.870
Right?

00:20:41.870 --> 00:20:44.510
So you're going to
give your VM a name.

00:20:44.510 --> 00:20:46.170
You're going to
give your VM a type.

00:20:46.170 --> 00:20:46.670
Right?

00:20:46.670 --> 00:20:48.472
So whether it's N1
standard and F1 micro,

00:20:48.472 --> 00:20:50.180
if you've done anything
in Compute Engine

00:20:50.180 --> 00:20:53.930
these are all things
that are familiar to you.

00:20:53.930 --> 00:20:56.810
You can set things like
boot disks, identity

00:20:56.810 --> 00:20:57.790
and access management.

00:20:57.790 --> 00:20:59.082
These are the service accounts.

00:20:59.082 --> 00:21:00.190
All this stuff can be set.

00:21:00.190 --> 00:21:02.900
I'm not going to go too
much into detail on these.

00:21:02.900 --> 00:21:04.490
You can set automatic
firewall rules

00:21:04.490 --> 00:21:07.940
that will be created to
allow HTTP and HTTPS.

00:21:07.940 --> 00:21:10.040
And then you've got this
ability to go in here

00:21:10.040 --> 00:21:12.370
and sort of define some of
the networking parameters.

00:21:12.370 --> 00:21:14.370
So I'm going to go
specifically into networking.

00:21:14.370 --> 00:21:16.140
So in this case, I've
created a network.

00:21:16.140 --> 00:21:18.410
In this case, it's load
balanced demo network.

00:21:18.410 --> 00:21:20.705
So I would pick that.

00:21:20.705 --> 00:21:22.955
And then I would pick a
subnet that's in that network.

00:21:22.955 --> 00:21:23.570
Right?

00:21:23.570 --> 00:21:25.640
So what I'm telling
it is, all these VMs

00:21:25.640 --> 00:21:28.910
are going to live in that
VPC, in that particular subnet

00:21:28.910 --> 00:21:29.660
that I've created.

00:21:29.660 --> 00:21:30.140
STEPHANIE WONG: OK.

00:21:30.140 --> 00:21:32.720
Can you actually have a
managed instance group with VMs

00:21:32.720 --> 00:21:34.593
in different regions?

00:21:34.593 --> 00:21:36.260
RYAN PRZYBYL: You
could run things where

00:21:36.260 --> 00:21:39.370
they're in different regions.

00:21:39.370 --> 00:21:41.370
In this case, I set
everything up in US central.

00:21:41.370 --> 00:21:41.650
Right?

00:21:41.650 --> 00:21:43.230
Because this is a
global load balancer.

00:21:43.230 --> 00:21:44.250
So you can build
those back ends.

00:21:44.250 --> 00:21:45.950
As I was saying, you can
build the instance group

00:21:45.950 --> 00:21:47.050
so it actually spans--

00:21:47.050 --> 00:21:47.510
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:21:47.510 --> 00:21:48.590
RYAN PRZYBYL: You'd actually
build probably an instance

00:21:48.590 --> 00:21:49.700
group in like the
central region.

00:21:49.700 --> 00:21:51.060
Another instance group
in the west region.

00:21:51.060 --> 00:21:51.266
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:21:51.266 --> 00:21:51.680
OK.

00:21:51.680 --> 00:21:53.280
Individual instance groups
in different regions.

00:21:53.280 --> 00:21:54.655
And have the global
load balancer

00:21:54.655 --> 00:21:56.010
distribute between those.

00:21:56.010 --> 00:21:56.870
RYAN PRZYBYL: Yep.

00:21:56.870 --> 00:21:59.328
So there was a whole bunch of
other configurations in here.

00:21:59.328 --> 00:22:01.790
I'm not going to go through
every one of these in depth.

00:22:01.790 --> 00:22:03.880
You can play with
these at your will.

00:22:03.880 --> 00:22:05.940
But this is how you
create a template.

00:22:05.940 --> 00:22:07.840
So, again, this is
sort of the first step.

00:22:07.840 --> 00:22:09.310
Right?

00:22:09.310 --> 00:22:10.340
So I'm going to go back.

00:22:10.340 --> 00:22:13.950
So this is the specific template
that I built in this case.

00:22:13.950 --> 00:22:16.670
So you can see I'm
running an N1 standard.

00:22:16.670 --> 00:22:19.970
It shows it's in
use by something.

00:22:19.970 --> 00:22:21.830
I have some firewall
rules set up for it.

00:22:21.830 --> 00:22:24.072
It's got an ephemeral
external IP address.

00:22:24.072 --> 00:22:26.030
Service tiers I'll talk
about in just a minute,

00:22:26.030 --> 00:22:27.540
whether it's premium or not.

00:22:27.540 --> 00:22:28.580
So these are all
the configurations

00:22:28.580 --> 00:22:29.663
I just sort of showed you.

00:22:29.663 --> 00:22:32.180
But this is the instance
template that I used.

00:22:32.180 --> 00:22:34.430
So once you have an
instance template,

00:22:34.430 --> 00:22:38.400
now we're going to create the
managed instance group itself.

00:22:38.400 --> 00:22:40.810
So here's the managed
instance group that I created,

00:22:40.810 --> 00:22:43.980
but to create your own you go up
here and create instance group.

00:22:43.980 --> 00:22:47.188
So you have the option
of managed or unmanaged.

00:22:47.188 --> 00:22:48.980
I talked about things
like health checking.

00:22:48.980 --> 00:22:50.340
I talk about things
like auto scaling.

00:22:50.340 --> 00:22:51.923
I talk about things
like auto healing.

00:22:51.923 --> 00:22:53.860
That's all part of the
managed instance group,

00:22:53.860 --> 00:22:56.360
because you're letting Google
manage all that stuff for you.

00:22:56.360 --> 00:22:56.860
Right?

00:22:56.860 --> 00:22:59.140
So in this case, I created
a managed instance group.

00:22:59.140 --> 00:23:01.850
So I'm going to give
it a name, whether I

00:23:01.850 --> 00:23:04.980
want it to live in a single
zone or multiple zones.

00:23:04.980 --> 00:23:08.936
So in my case, I configured
something in US central one.

00:23:08.936 --> 00:23:11.186
You can configure various
zones in there, the A, B, C,

00:23:11.186 --> 00:23:15.260
and D redundancy zones.

00:23:15.260 --> 00:23:17.600
And then here you see it
requires instance template.

00:23:17.600 --> 00:23:18.650
This is why I had to
build the instance

00:23:18.650 --> 00:23:20.440
template before I could build
the managed instance group.

00:23:20.440 --> 00:23:20.594
STEPHANIE WONG: OK.

00:23:20.594 --> 00:23:20.750
Got it.

00:23:20.750 --> 00:23:22.490
RYAN PRZYBYL: So here is where
I would grab that template

00:23:22.490 --> 00:23:24.278
that I just built, click on it.

00:23:24.278 --> 00:23:26.570
Say this is the template I
want you to use as you build

00:23:26.570 --> 00:23:29.277
and scale this managed
instance group.

00:23:29.277 --> 00:23:31.610
Auto scaling, because I've
got a managed instance group.

00:23:31.610 --> 00:23:32.960
So I'm setting auto scaling on.

00:23:32.960 --> 00:23:34.965
It's auto scaling based
on CPU utilization.

00:23:34.965 --> 00:23:36.840
But there's different
things that you can do.

00:23:36.840 --> 00:23:38.798
So in this case, I just
picked CPU utilization.

00:23:38.798 --> 00:23:40.230
I specify the percentage.

00:23:40.230 --> 00:23:42.240
So once it exceeds
an aggregate--

00:23:42.240 --> 00:23:44.360
so let's say I'm
running three VMs--

00:23:44.360 --> 00:23:47.417
once the aggregate CPU
utilization exceeds 60%,

00:23:47.417 --> 00:23:49.500
it will spin up another
one, spin up a fourth one,

00:23:49.500 --> 00:23:52.160
and add it to the pool.

00:23:52.160 --> 00:23:54.830
In this case, I've told it the
maximum number of instances

00:23:54.830 --> 00:23:56.270
I want to run is 10.

00:23:56.270 --> 00:23:59.548
So I might say here, let's say
a minimum of 3, maximum of 10.

00:23:59.548 --> 00:24:00.340
STEPHANIE WONG: OK.

00:24:00.340 --> 00:24:02.960
Good to know so you don't wake
up and there's 1,000 running.

00:24:02.960 --> 00:24:04.163
RYAN PRZYBYL: Yep.

00:24:04.163 --> 00:24:06.080
The cool down period is
actually how much time

00:24:06.080 --> 00:24:07.122
it takes to spin this up.

00:24:07.122 --> 00:24:10.030
So depending on your VM and
what you have configured on it,

00:24:10.030 --> 00:24:11.420
so if you're running a
bunch of startup scripts,

00:24:11.420 --> 00:24:14.087
or you're bootstrapping that, or
you're loading a bunch of stuff

00:24:14.087 --> 00:24:16.190
on the VM, the VM could
take longer to start up.

00:24:16.190 --> 00:24:18.470
So this is effectively
how long you

00:24:18.470 --> 00:24:20.810
want it to wait before it
starts polling that thing

00:24:20.810 --> 00:24:22.060
to get information out of it.

00:24:22.060 --> 00:24:22.230
Right?

00:24:22.230 --> 00:24:22.580
STEPHANIE WONG: Oh, OK.

00:24:22.580 --> 00:24:25.440
RYAN PRZYBYL: So right now the
default is set for 60 seconds.

00:24:25.440 --> 00:24:26.982
And then I talked
about auto healing.

00:24:26.982 --> 00:24:29.450
So this is, like I mentioned,
if the device were to fail,

00:24:29.450 --> 00:24:32.180
so if the VM fails, so one
