00:36:01.520 --> 00:36:02.062
Thanks again.

00:36:38.330 --> 00:36:38.830
All right.

00:36:38.830 --> 00:36:40.570
So we're back with some FAQ.

00:36:40.570 --> 00:36:43.210
The first being whether
our load balance

00:36:43.210 --> 00:36:45.333
actually require pre
warming to scale.

00:36:45.333 --> 00:36:47.750
Because I know that's a concern
that some people may have.

00:36:47.750 --> 00:36:47.900
RYAN PRZYBYL: Yeah.

00:36:47.900 --> 00:36:48.860
We get that question a lot.

00:36:48.860 --> 00:36:50.710
And some of that comes
from legacy products that

00:36:50.710 --> 00:36:51.480
are out there in the industry.

00:36:51.480 --> 00:36:51.980
Right?

00:36:51.980 --> 00:36:53.470
Where you've got
to define things.

00:36:53.470 --> 00:36:54.740
Right?

00:36:54.740 --> 00:36:57.770
I think the key answer
is, no, you don't have to.

00:36:57.770 --> 00:36:58.270
Right?

00:36:58.270 --> 00:37:00.820
But part of this is because,
again, we built these load

00:37:00.820 --> 00:37:03.547
balancers as not a
product for cloud to sell,

00:37:03.547 --> 00:37:05.630
but for things like our
search engine to function.

00:37:05.630 --> 00:37:08.920
So as you can imagine, if
there was a disaster someplace

00:37:08.920 --> 00:37:12.170
in the world the next 10
seconds, I can't predict that.

00:37:12.170 --> 00:37:12.670
Right?

00:37:12.670 --> 00:37:12.940
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:37:12.940 --> 00:37:14.320
RYAN PRZYBYL: And we
would get just tons

00:37:14.320 --> 00:37:16.308
of searches of what's
going on in country X

00:37:16.308 --> 00:37:17.100
that just happened.

00:37:17.100 --> 00:37:18.550
Or I heard something
great on the news

00:37:18.550 --> 00:37:19.390
that I need to research.

00:37:19.390 --> 00:37:19.930
Right?

00:37:19.930 --> 00:37:22.230
So it would be impossible
for us to sort of do

00:37:22.230 --> 00:37:23.230
any sort of pre warming.

00:37:23.230 --> 00:37:23.730
Right?

00:37:23.730 --> 00:37:27.160
So this is why these things
that were built as a product,

00:37:27.160 --> 00:37:28.910
they have some of those
limitations in it.

00:37:28.910 --> 00:37:30.702
But when we built our
global load balancer,

00:37:30.702 --> 00:37:32.540
this just wouldn't
have worked for us.

00:37:32.540 --> 00:37:33.670
It just wouldn't have worked
for our search engine.

00:37:33.670 --> 00:37:35.555
It wouldn't have worked for
YouTube when things go viral.

00:37:35.555 --> 00:37:35.880
Right?

00:37:35.880 --> 00:37:36.620
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:37:36.620 --> 00:37:38.050
RYAN PRZYBYL: So the
ultimate answer is, no.

00:37:38.050 --> 00:37:38.910
You don't have to do it.

00:37:38.910 --> 00:37:40.720
But a lot of it's because just
the Google engineering that

00:37:40.720 --> 00:37:42.220
went into this, and
how this was not

00:37:42.220 --> 00:37:44.860
developed as a product for
cloud, but rather something

00:37:44.860 --> 00:37:47.840
we use to just make Google's
infrastructure work.

00:37:47.840 --> 00:37:48.340
Right?

00:37:48.340 --> 00:37:50.240
It is that sort of
linchpin device for us.

00:37:50.240 --> 00:37:50.410
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:37:50.410 --> 00:37:52.285
And I think you mentioned
in the presentation

00:37:52.285 --> 00:37:55.645
that we're straying away
from focusing or basing it

00:37:55.645 --> 00:37:56.650
off a DNS approach.

00:37:56.650 --> 00:37:58.920
We're using our global
front end for the external.

00:37:58.920 --> 00:38:01.090
For the internal,
it's not proxy based.

00:38:01.090 --> 00:38:03.050
It's using our software
defined networking.

00:38:03.050 --> 00:38:06.413
So that's kind of what enables
us to be able to not require

00:38:06.413 --> 00:38:07.080
the pre warming.

00:38:07.080 --> 00:38:09.680
It's not based on physical
servers, as we talked about.

00:38:09.680 --> 00:38:10.430
RYAN PRZYBYL: Yep.

00:38:10.430 --> 00:38:11.050
STEPHANIE WONG: So the next one.

00:38:11.050 --> 00:38:11.230
OK.

00:38:11.230 --> 00:38:13.510
So we mentioned premium
versus standard networking.

00:38:13.510 --> 00:38:16.990
And you talked about how premium
utilizes the Google network

00:38:16.990 --> 00:38:17.590
backbone.

00:38:17.590 --> 00:38:19.720
And it gives us higher
bandwidth and throughput.

00:38:19.720 --> 00:38:21.260
And standard is
for cost savings.

00:38:21.260 --> 00:38:23.410
So for the front
end specifically,

00:38:23.410 --> 00:38:26.020
front end load balancer,
can you do premium tier

00:38:26.020 --> 00:38:27.778
for both global and regional?

00:38:27.778 --> 00:38:28.570
RYAN PRZYBYL: Yeah.

00:38:28.570 --> 00:38:31.228
You can set up either
premium or standard

00:38:31.228 --> 00:38:32.520
tier for either load balancers.

00:38:32.520 --> 00:38:33.020
Right?

00:38:33.020 --> 00:38:35.320
And like I said, it's
really done to sort of cost

00:38:35.320 --> 00:38:36.880
manage traffic
more than anything.

00:38:36.880 --> 00:38:37.380
Right?

00:38:37.380 --> 00:38:39.050
Google has built this
great big network.

00:38:39.050 --> 00:38:40.090
And we want you to
be able to use it.

00:38:40.090 --> 00:38:40.590
Right?

00:38:40.590 --> 00:38:43.150
So we love for people to use
premium tier, because it just

00:38:43.150 --> 00:38:45.700
gives a better user experience,
in our view of the world.

00:38:45.700 --> 00:38:46.330
Right?

00:38:46.330 --> 00:38:47.890
But we also understand
that customers

00:38:47.890 --> 00:38:49.780
are trying to cost
optimize traffic sometimes.

00:38:49.780 --> 00:38:50.280
Right?

00:38:50.280 --> 00:38:52.938
And so that's why we created
sort of that standard tier,

00:38:52.938 --> 00:38:54.230
where you can say, hey, Google.

00:38:54.230 --> 00:38:55.090
You have this great backbone.

00:38:55.090 --> 00:38:56.290
But it costs more
money than they

00:38:56.290 --> 00:38:57.540
want to spend on this traffic.

00:38:57.540 --> 00:39:00.220
So dump it off to the internet
as quickly as you possibly can.

00:39:00.220 --> 00:39:02.080
And let it sort of go
through the internet,

00:39:02.080 --> 00:39:03.780
and go through all of the
cost structure that is

00:39:03.780 --> 00:39:05.080
very complex in terms of that.

00:39:05.080 --> 00:39:05.395
Right?

00:39:05.395 --> 00:39:05.710
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:39:05.710 --> 00:39:06.940
Depending on the use case.

00:39:06.940 --> 00:39:07.732
RYAN PRZYBYL: Yeah.

00:39:07.732 --> 00:39:10.510
But you can set it up in a
lot of the different fashions.

00:39:10.510 --> 00:39:12.070
You'll see premium
versus standard

00:39:12.070 --> 00:39:13.340
in a lot of the load
balancer configurations.

00:39:13.340 --> 00:39:13.960
Right?

00:39:13.960 --> 00:39:15.460
Specifically, the
external facing ones.

00:39:15.460 --> 00:39:16.510
Because the internal
ones, remember,

00:39:16.510 --> 00:39:17.980
it's all just
riding on Google's.

00:39:17.980 --> 00:39:19.480
It's meant to be inside
of what you can see.

00:39:19.480 --> 00:39:21.100
So it's all riding
internal to Google.

00:39:21.100 --> 00:39:21.400
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:39:21.400 --> 00:39:24.067
RYAN PRZYBYL: It's not something
that ever touches the internet.

00:39:24.067 --> 00:39:26.403
So as I said early on
in the first slide,

00:39:26.403 --> 00:39:27.820
you can think of
internal services

00:39:27.820 --> 00:39:28.990
versus external services.

00:39:28.990 --> 00:39:31.328
The premium standard only
applies to external load

00:39:31.328 --> 00:39:32.120
balancing services.

00:39:32.120 --> 00:39:32.520
STEPHANIE WONG: OK.

00:39:32.520 --> 00:39:33.020
Understood.

00:39:33.020 --> 00:39:33.840
Great.

00:39:33.840 --> 00:39:34.340
Next one.

00:39:34.340 --> 00:39:37.090
So the load balancer
is high availability,

00:39:37.090 --> 00:39:38.050
or highly available.

00:39:38.050 --> 00:39:41.360
What can you do to increase the
availability of your back ends?

00:39:41.360 --> 00:39:42.460
RYAN PRZYBYL: So this is
where the managed instance

00:39:42.460 --> 00:39:43.030
groups come in.

00:39:43.030 --> 00:39:43.360
Right?

00:39:43.360 --> 00:39:45.520
This is the whole point of
the managed instance groups.

00:39:45.520 --> 00:39:45.730
Right?

00:39:45.730 --> 00:39:48.060
You're building highly
available and highly redundant

00:39:48.060 --> 00:39:49.060
managed instance groups.

00:39:49.060 --> 00:39:49.720
Right?

00:39:49.720 --> 00:39:51.387
So as I was talking
about with the GCLB,

00:39:51.387 --> 00:39:53.803
you could build managed instance
groups into every region,

00:39:53.803 --> 00:39:55.150
and it'll find the closest one.

00:39:55.150 --> 00:39:56.117
Right?

00:39:56.117 --> 00:39:58.450
And that's what gives you
that sort of highly available,

00:39:58.450 --> 00:39:59.470
highly scalable--

00:39:59.470 --> 00:40:01.480
I like to talk about
Black Friday as a good use

00:40:01.480 --> 00:40:02.150
case for this.

00:40:02.150 --> 00:40:02.750
Right?

00:40:02.750 --> 00:40:04.720
If you're in the
retail industry,

00:40:04.720 --> 00:40:06.340
if you do anything
versus purchasing,

00:40:06.340 --> 00:40:08.060
that is the day you
have to engineer around.

00:40:08.060 --> 00:40:08.250
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:40:08.250 --> 00:40:08.430
RYAN PRZYBYL: Right?

00:40:08.430 --> 00:40:10.243
Everybody engineers
around Black Friday.

00:40:10.243 --> 00:40:11.410
But that doesn't make sense.

00:40:11.410 --> 00:40:11.690
Right?

00:40:11.690 --> 00:40:13.232
Why do you want to
run 1,000 servers,

00:40:13.232 --> 00:40:16.330
or why do you want to run 1,000
load balancers that get used,

00:40:16.330 --> 00:40:17.470
call it one week a year.

00:40:17.470 --> 00:40:17.740
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:40:17.740 --> 00:40:18.400
RYAN PRZYBYL: Right?

00:40:18.400 --> 00:40:20.567
So that's where the managed
instance groups come in.

00:40:20.567 --> 00:40:22.960
It gives you that scalability
and that protection

00:40:22.960 --> 00:40:24.760
from a highly
available standpoint

00:40:24.760 --> 00:40:27.070
where if something fails,
we spin it back up.

00:40:27.070 --> 00:40:28.735
Cause you're letting Google
manage that infrastructure.

00:40:28.735 --> 00:40:29.050
Right?

00:40:29.050 --> 00:40:29.660
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:40:29.660 --> 00:40:30.880
RYAN PRZYBYL: So
that whole concept

00:40:30.880 --> 00:40:32.672
of using a managed
instance group is really

00:40:32.672 --> 00:40:36.160
designed around making
highly available back end

00:40:36.160 --> 00:40:36.890
infrastructure.

00:40:36.890 --> 00:40:37.570
STEPHANIE WONG: Awesome.

00:40:37.570 --> 00:40:38.100
Great.

00:40:38.100 --> 00:40:39.302
OK.

00:40:39.302 --> 00:40:40.510
I think this is the last one.

00:40:40.510 --> 00:40:43.300
How about connection draining
for back end instances

00:40:43.300 --> 00:40:45.290
to reduce disruption
to end users?

00:40:45.290 --> 00:40:48.490
Like let's say you want to move
from one back end to another

00:40:48.490 --> 00:40:51.380
without any kind of poor
experience for the user.

00:40:51.380 --> 00:40:52.130
RYAN PRZYBYL: Yep.

00:40:52.130 --> 00:40:53.650
So this is another
common question.

00:40:53.650 --> 00:40:54.150
Right?

00:40:54.150 --> 00:40:56.570
So when we do auto scaling
and things scale up.

00:40:56.570 --> 00:40:58.240
So let's use our
Black Friday example.

00:40:58.240 --> 00:40:58.750
Right?

00:40:58.750 --> 00:40:59.560
Midnight happens.

00:40:59.560 --> 00:41:00.577
A sale goes on.

00:41:00.577 --> 00:41:02.410
And you get bombarded
with all this traffic.

00:41:02.410 --> 00:41:05.170
So you scale up to 10
servers on the backside.

00:41:05.170 --> 00:41:05.830
Right?

00:41:05.830 --> 00:41:09.070
There is a variable
in the configuration

00:41:09.070 --> 00:41:10.840
that I didn't show
that basically says,

00:41:10.840 --> 00:41:12.130
what is the draining period.

00:41:12.130 --> 00:41:12.630
Right?

00:41:12.630 --> 00:41:14.518
So I think it defaults
to 300 seconds.

00:41:14.518 --> 00:41:15.310
STEPHANIE WONG: OK.

00:41:15.310 --> 00:41:16.935
RYAN PRZYBYL: So
what's going to happen

00:41:16.935 --> 00:41:19.090
is, as traffic
starts to ramp down,

00:41:19.090 --> 00:41:21.310
so let's say maybe 4
o'clock in the morning,

00:41:21.310 --> 00:41:23.090
you got your initial barrage.

00:41:23.090 --> 00:41:25.810
You scaled up to
meet that demand.

00:41:25.810 --> 00:41:27.720
Now things start to
sort of fall off.

00:41:27.720 --> 00:41:28.480
Right?

00:41:28.480 --> 00:41:31.180
When it sees the utilization
go down, what it's going to do

00:41:31.180 --> 00:41:32.500
is it's going to say, OK.

00:41:32.500 --> 00:41:35.790
All new connections, I'm
going to drain this back end.

00:41:35.790 --> 00:41:36.290
Right?

00:41:36.290 --> 00:41:38.130
So I'm not going to put any
new connections on there.

00:41:38.130 --> 00:41:40.380
I'm going to wait 300
seconds before I take it out

00:41:40.380 --> 00:41:40.920
of the pool.

00:41:40.920 --> 00:41:41.260
Right?

00:41:41.260 --> 00:41:41.640
STEPHANIE WONG: OK.

00:41:41.640 --> 00:41:42.565
RYAN PRZYBYL: So
let sessions finish.

00:41:42.565 --> 00:41:43.860
Let sessions drain off of it.

00:41:43.860 --> 00:41:46.110
And then, because it's not
putting any new ones on it,

00:41:46.110 --> 00:41:48.570
it's putting new ones
over here, once it's down,

00:41:48.570 --> 00:41:49.710
it'll pull that one down.

00:41:49.710 --> 00:41:51.617
And it'll keep doing
that and scaling down

00:41:51.617 --> 00:41:52.575
as your load decreases.

00:41:52.575 --> 00:41:53.140
STEPHANIE WONG: That's great.

00:41:53.140 --> 00:41:55.440
Because then they don't
experience a just cut off,

00:41:55.440 --> 00:41:57.430
and then we're still kind of
spinning up this instance.

00:41:57.430 --> 00:41:58.030
It's directing traffic.

00:41:58.030 --> 00:41:58.160
RYAN PRZYBYL: Yeah.

00:41:58.160 --> 00:42:00.493
It's very intelligent in terms
of like it knows, oh, I'm

00:42:00.493 --> 00:42:03.460
in a cool down period where I
need to scale this stuff back.

00:42:03.460 --> 00:42:05.860
And it does so in a
very intelligent way.

00:42:05.860 --> 00:42:08.160
It doesn't just say, oh,
I'm going to go back to two.

00:42:08.160 --> 00:42:10.460
Dump all these other five
that I spun up, and strand

00:42:10.460 --> 00:42:10.910
all these customers.

00:42:10.910 --> 00:42:11.000
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:42:11.000 --> 00:42:13.110
So it's slowly redirecting
traffic and users

00:42:13.110 --> 00:42:13.520
to the other one.

00:42:13.520 --> 00:42:13.750
RYAN PRZYBYL: Yep.

00:42:13.750 --> 00:42:14.000
STEPHANIE WONG: That's great.

00:42:14.000 --> 00:42:14.700
RYAN PRZYBYL: And
like I said, you

00:42:14.700 --> 00:42:16.560
configure that
sort of stuff when

00:42:16.560 --> 00:42:17.768
you're setting this stuff up.

00:42:17.768 --> 00:42:18.768
STEPHANIE WONG: Awesome.

00:42:18.768 --> 00:42:19.715
This is super helpful.

00:42:19.715 --> 00:42:21.390
And thank you so
much again, Ryan.

00:42:21.390 --> 00:42:23.490
Thank you, everyone,
for tuning in today.

00:42:23.490 --> 00:42:25.060
And again, don't forget.

00:42:25.060 --> 00:42:27.750
Please visit the
Cloud OnAir website

00:42:27.750 --> 00:42:30.040
for more content from
Google Cloud experts.

00:42:30.040 --> 00:42:31.470
And we'll see you
